id: records/2026-09-04-advocate-challenger-ranked-move-clarification
title: "Advocate and challenger as a deliberation aperture over ranked moves"
kind: clarification-record
status: proposed-canonical-backfill
recorded: 2026-09-04
supersedes_history: false
mutationAuthorized: false
externalEffectsAuthorized: false

source_observation: >
  The root attention frontier should not present an unexplained next-best-move
  score. Selecting a ranked item should progressively disclose why it currently
  leads, the strongest advocate and adversarial cases, the assumptions and
  evidence behind the estimate, the unresolved lacunae, and the changes that
  would alter the ranking. A market-like trace can make belief revision visible
  as the user adds information.

clarification:
  central_move: >
    Treat advocate and adversary/challenger as attributed roles inside one
    bounded move-deliberation episode, not as automatic persistent Yawnbots.
  persistence_boundary: >
    A Yawn.bot is the durable runtime around a Yawn. A temporary advocate or
    challenger is normally an ephemeral Agent role with bounded context, budget,
    authority, output, and expiry. It earns a persistent Yawn only if its work
    develops an independently meaningful orientation and proof loop.
  principal: >
    The ranking engine and integrator produce a recommendation hypothesis. The
    rightful principal may accept, modify, reject, hold, or delegate it. User
    agreement is not proof that the recommendation was correct.
  reality: >
    Advocate and challenger help before action. Consequence and proof constrain
    the model afterward. Neither model consensus nor eloquent argument replaces
    reality contact.

pressure_test_corrections:
  - >
    Do not force a symmetric debate. Steelman both sides while allowing evidence
    to remain strongly asymmetric; otherwise the interface manufactures false
    balance.
  - >
    Do not use one opaque line for probability, leverage, importance, salience,
    and rank. These quantities must remain distinguishable.
  - >
    A Polymarket-like line is a forecast-revision trace, not a market price. It
    must show whose estimate it is, what outcome and horizon it concerns, which
    evidence moved it, and where no estimate was recorded.
  - >
    One model producing both sides is useful but correlated. High-consequence
    decisions may justify blinded or cross-provider independent Agents.
  - >
    More role Agents are not automatically more intelligence. Spawn only when
    expected information gain exceeds compute, coordination, and attention cost.
  - >
    A user's click teaches preferences or reveals missing context; it does not
    establish that the clicked move was objectively best. Outcome and proof must
    remain separate learning signals.

ui_aperture:
  collapsed:
    - current_rank
    - candidate_move
    - why_now
    - outcome_probability_or_unknown
    - expected_leverage_summary
    - rank_confidence
    - largest_lacuna
  expanded:
    - intended_outcome_and_time_horizon
    - advocate_case
    - challenger_case
    - assumptions
    - supporting_and_contradictory_evidence
    - factor_contributions
    - forecast_history
    - rank_history
    - alternatives_and_opportunity_cost
    - what_would_change_the_ranking
    - authority_and_provisioning
    - dialogue
    - accept_modify_reject_hold_or_delegate

learning_loop:
  sequence:
    - ranked_candidate_set
    - advocate_and_challenger_cases
    - rationale_dialogue
    - new_attributed_user_or_world_evidence
    - explicit_ranking_revision_with_delta
    - principal_choice_or_hold
    - move_or_delegation
    - consequence
    - proof_evaluation
    - calibration_update
  desired_behavior: >
    As the user contributes information, YAWN should identify which assumption,
    relationship, value, constraint, probability, or dependency changed; revise
    only the affected estimate; preserve the prior estimate; and explain the
    delta. Repeated corrections may improve local ranking policy, while broader
    propagation requires stronger evidence and authority.

root_attention_frontier:
  rule: >
    The username/root Yawn renders only a small current stack rank across local
    Yawn attention claims. Opening an item moves into its deliberation aperture;
    it does not flatten the underlying holarchy or convert View order into a
    permanent containment hierarchy.

implementation_target:
  statement: >
    Clicking a ranked next move should feel like opening the market and argument
    structure behind the recommendation: what outcome is being forecast, who is
    making each case, why the estimate moved, what remains unknown, and what the
    principal can do next.
  public_boundary: >
    Public Views may show a sanitized candidate and rationale while private
    evidence, health data, relationship history, and agent traces remain hidden
    under effective field-level provisioning.

proof_questions:
  - "Does opening the advocate/challenger aperture improve decision understanding or merely add more prose?"
  - "Do user corrections produce traceable rank changes and fewer repeated model errors?"
  - "Are probability forecasts calibrated separately from leverage and priority rank?"
  - "Does independent challenge improve outcomes enough to justify its compute and attention cost?"
  - "Can historical replay reconstruct why a move was ranked first at that moment?"
  - "Does the system preserve strong evidence asymmetry instead of forcing both sides to appear equal?"

